From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 22:02:43 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 173/194] hjviewer: Auto Login types the Windows password only
 at the logon or lock screen

With Auto Login on, the controller typed the saved Windows password and
Return into the PC on every connect and reconnect without knowing whether
the PC was locked. On an unlocked desktop the password landed in whatever
window had the focus: a chat box, a search field, a document. The
controller now clicks and waits as before and then hands the password to
the PC in the new Message.os_login (field 34, free in our proto); the PC
looks for its logon or lock screen at that moment and types the password
and Return only there, otherwise it drops them. The PC accepts os_login
only in a remote-screen session with keyboard permission. The "OS
Password" menu item still types at once.

Backported from rustdesk/rustdesk 13e1278b4c46eadede9546d3ada1ff9d94b593dd (fix: auto-login types the OS password only at the logon or lock screen (#16520))
One change against upstream's diff: the gate. Upstream sends os_login to
Windows and macOS peers from "1.5.1", but an HJViewer PC reports
HJViewer's own version (scripts/set-client-version.sh writes
client/version.env into Cargo.toml, build.rs makes crate::VERSION of it
and the login answer's PeerInfo.version is that), so the released 1.9.2
PCs would pass upstream's gate, get a message they do not know and type
nothing. The gate is HJViewer 1.9.3, the first PC with the handler: a
1.9.3 controller keeps typing the keys itself into a 1.9.2 PC. A test
holds the gate with our version numbers.

Windows is_locked() now reads the session's lock flag through the new
is_session_locked_flag(): the same answer as before on Windows 8 and
later, and the right one on Windows 7, which reports the flag reversed.
Our end-of-session lock (src/server/connection/hjv_end_lock.rs,
locked_now) reads is_locked() for "was the PC locked when the session
came in" and needs no change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
